            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Matrix multiplication: each row of the first input combined with each column of the second. Turning points with a rotation matrix, applying a transform, mixing channels with a weight matrix, projecting data onto principal axes - all are one t.matmul.\n\nA stack is handled in one step: give it 24 rotation matrices (shape 24 x 3 x 3) and 24 vectors (24 x 3 x 1) and every joint is turned by its own matrix at once. A single matrix as the second input is applied to every matrix of a stack.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.matmul\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe left matrix (or stack). Every input multiplies and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe right matrix (or stack), held. Its last-but-one size must equal in 1's last size. It is converted to in 1's device and type.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe product. Whole numbers come out as floats.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points, one per row: right, up, left, down. Times the matrix 0 1, -1 0 - a quarter turn - each point moves a quarter of the way round, counter-clockwise: right becomes up, up becomes left.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.matmul is the numpy version. t.einsum writes any product of this kind as a formula. t.linalg.inv undoes a matrix.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Matrix multiplication: each row of the first input combined with each column of the second. Turning points with a rotation matrix, applying a transform, mixing channels with a weight matrix, projecting data onto principal axes - all are one t.matmul.\n\nA stack is handled in one step: give it 24 rotation matrices (shape 24 x 3 x 3) and 24 vectors (24 x 3 x 1) and every joint is turned by its own matrix at once. A single matrix as the second input is applied to every matrix of a stack.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.matmul\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe left matrix (or stack). Every input multiplies and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe right matrix (or stack), held. Its last-but-one size must equal in 1's last size. It is converted to in 1's device and type.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe product. Whole numbers come out as floats.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points, one per row: right, up, left, down. Times the matrix 0 1, -1 0 - a quarter turn - each point moves a quarter of the way round, counter-clockwise: right becomes up, up becomes left.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.matmul is the numpy version. t.einsum writes any product of this kind as a formula. t.linalg.inv undoes a matrix."
        },
